.TH ORCH 1 "2026-10-25" "orch" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
orch \- inspect and uninstall command-line tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B orch list
[\fB\-\-native\fR | \fB\-\-user\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
.br
.B orch info
.I name
.br
.B orch uninstall
.I name
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-y\fR | \fB\-\-yes\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B orch
scans the directories in
.B PATH
and marks every executable it finds as
.I native
(shipped with the operating system) or
.I user
(installed by you). It can show details about a tool and uninstall it through the package manager that owns it.
.PP
.B orch
is not a package manager. It delegates uninstalls to one that is already installed (currently Homebrew) and never deletes files itself.
.PP
Only macOS is supported.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B list
List tools found on
.BR PATH .
When a name appears in several directories, the first one wins. Columns are name, kind, source and path. Source is a guess made from the resolved path: brew, cargo, go, npm, manual or unknown.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-native
Show only tools shipped with the OS.
.TP
.B \-\-user
Show only user-installed tools.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON instead of a table.
.RE
.TP
\fBinfo\fR \fIname\fR
Show path, resolved path, kind, source, owning package manager, version and the one-line man page description.
.IP
The version is read by running \fIname\fR \fB\-\-version\fR with a 2 second timeout, which executes the tool.
.TP
\fBuninstall\fR \fIname\fR
Uninstall a tool with the package manager that owns it. The command is printed first and a confirmation is requested.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the command and exit without running it.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.RE
.SH SAFETY
.B uninstall
refuses native OS tools. If no package manager owns the tool, it prints the path and exits with an error so you can remove the file yourself.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Tool not found, native tool refused, no owner, aborted, or the package manager failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List tools you installed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
orch list \-\-user
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what would be run to remove a Homebrew tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
orch uninstall jq \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /opt/homebrew/Cellar
Used to detect Homebrew ownership from the resolved path.
